\section{A Diagram Is Syntax Only After Its Rules Are Known}

LabVIEW programs are virtual instruments (VIs) with a front panel and a block diagram. Wires connect terminals; a node can execute when required inputs are available, while loops, structures, references, queues, and I/O add state and effects. The visual placement of two nodes does not impose left-to-right execution. Conversely, a sequence structure can impose order even when there is no ordinary value dependence. The survey by Johnston, Hanna, and Millar treats LabVIEW as an important visual dataflow environment while also documenting that visual dataflow languages vary in their execution models \cite{johnston2004dataflow}.

A Formula Node embeds textual syntax inside the visual graph. A representative node body is:

\begin{verbatim}
y = (a * x) + b;
if (y < 0) y = 0;
\end{verbatim}

That text has sequential statement semantics inside the node. It does not mean the surrounding VI is a C program, and ordinary wire crossings do not imply connection unless the editor's graph says so. A compiler should consume the saved graph's object and terminal identities, not reconstruct connectivity from pixels.

\section{Lower Structured Pictures to Typed Regions}

The frontend can represent each node instance, typed terminal, wire net, structure tunnel, shift register, effect token, and source object ID. A loop becomes a region with explicit carried values; a case structure becomes alternative regions with matching result terminals. A subVI is a call with connector-pane bindings. Front-panel controls and indicators are boundary objects, not global variables inferred from labels.

\Cref{fig:visual-369} shows why an effect edge can be necessary even when two nodes have no value wire between them.

