\section{Compile a VI Without Inventing Visual Order}

\begin{verbatim}
objects, wires := decode_saved_vi(resource)
verify every wire joins compatible terminal directions and types
for each graphical structure:
    create region arguments for tunnels and loop-carried shift registers
lower nodes after their required value and effect inputs are available
schedule independent pure nodes freely inside each region
emit code plus object-ID-to-instruction debug map
\end{verbatim}

Constant folding inside the Formula Node and common-subexpression elimination between pure primitives are legal. Independent pure nodes may run in parallel. Loop-invariant motion must account for tunnels, shift registers, references, random generators, timing nodes, and I/O. An error wire may carry a value, but its practical ordering role must not be erased by dead-value elimination. Likewise, replacing a subVI call requires respecting reentrancy and hidden state.

Preservation tests compare terminal values, selected cases, loop iteration results, and ordered effect logs under the same inputs. The validator also checks region interfaces and ensures every emitted diagnostic maps to a surviving object ID. These checks distinguish semantic equivalence from layout preservation: an optimizer may rearrange or synthesize nodes internally, yet an editor-facing artifact should preserve enough provenance to highlight the original wire or terminal responsible for an error.

\section{Follow a Loop Value Through a Shift Register}

Consider a For Loop that sums an input array by auto-indexing its elements and carrying the total through an initialized shift register. Use initial state zero and input \(4,-1,8\). One wire brings the current element into the body and another supplies the old total. The outgoing total returns as the next iteration's old total, not as a value available earlier within the same iteration.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{crrr}
\hline
Iteration & Element tunnel & Old shift value & New shift value \\
\hline
0 & 4 & 0 & 4 \\
1 & -1 & 4 & 3 \\
2 & 8 & 3 & 11 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{verbatim}
lower_initialized_loop(diagram):
    count := array_extent(diagram.indexed_input)
    carry := lower(diagram.initial_shift_value)
    for i in 0 .. count-1:
        element := read(diagram.indexed_input, i)
        result := eval_region(diagram.body, element, carry)
        carry := result.next_shift_value
    return carry
\end{verbatim}

The zero-iteration case returns the initialized value without executing the region. Do not assume that an uninitialized shift register follows the same rule: LabVIEW can retain its value between calls. If a body also emits a log record through an error or dependency wire, represent that ordering as another region dependency rather than scheduling the log by its location on screen. For a compiler fixture, move the diagram objects without rewiring, then compare the old and new compiled observations. As a negative test, rewire the addition to use the initial constant instead of the shift input. The result becomes \(8\), and the diagnostic trace should identify the incoming wire whose meaning changed.

\paragraph{Preserve tunnel identities during edits.}
A region parameter should record the original structure and tunnel,
not just the nearest wire coordinate. Deleting and recreating a tunnel
at the same location should not reuse a cached type or binding from
the deleted object. Moving the same tunnel, by contrast, need not
invalidate a semantic analysis if its terminals and wiring are
unchanged. Check the distinction by assigning persistent object IDs
in one fixture and comparing the editor's highlighted terminal after
lowering reports a type mismatch.
